  <compounddef id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-suites" kind="group">
    <compoundname>micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-suites</compoundname>
    <title>Test suites</title>
    <innerclass refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1located__name" prot="public">micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::located_name</innerclass>
    <innerclass refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1static__suite" prot="public">micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::static_suite</innerclass>
    <innerclass refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1suite" prot="public">micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::suite</innerclass>
    <innerclass refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1top__suite" prot="public">micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::top_suite</innerclass>
    <briefdescription>
<para>Test suites are named groups of related test cases. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>A suite has a name and a body, a function that creates its test cases. The suites are either added in <computeroutput>main()</computeroutput> with <computeroutput>runner::add_suite()</computeroutput>, or defined as static suites, and <computeroutput>runner::run_suites()</computeroutput> runs them all, sorted by name.</para>
<para>The top suite is a shortcut: a suite whose body is the code of <computeroutput>main()</computeroutput> itself. <computeroutput>runner::add_top_suite()</computeroutput> adds it and returns it, and the test cases created on it in <computeroutput>main()</computeroutput> run immediately, before <computeroutput>runner::run_suites()</computeroutput> is called. It therefore always runs before all the other suites, whatever their names. It is optional; a program may put all its test cases in the other suites.</para>
<para>A test case can be created only on the suite whose body is running: on the top suite from <computeroutput>main()</computeroutput>, or on the suite received by the body of another suite. A call on another suite, for example on the top suite captured in the body of another suite, is reported as a failure, and the test case is not run.</para>
<para>The static suites are <computeroutput>mt::static_suite</computeroutput> objects, defined at namespace scope, usually in separate source files, and constructed with a name, a reference to the runner, and a callable (usually a function or a lambda with a <computeroutput>mt::suite&amp;</computeroutput> first parameter, which creates the test cases), along with any optional arguments. They register themselves in their constructors, so they are included in the test run without other calls.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="important"><para>The runner referred to by the static suites in other source files must have external linkage (not <computeroutput>static</computeroutput>), and be declared <computeroutput>extern</computeroutput> in a header shared by these files.</para>
</simplesect>
A static suite does not need the runner to be constructed first; it can be defined in any source file, before or after the runner.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Execution order</title><para>The top suite runs first, as described above. <computeroutput>runner::run_suites()</computeroutput> then runs all the other suites, the added and the static ones together, sorted by name:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>the names are compared byte by byte, as by <computeroutput>std::strcmp()</computeroutput>, so the upper-case letters come before the lower-case ones (<computeroutput>"Gamma"</computeroutput> runs before <computeroutput>"alpha"</computeroutput>);</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>suites with the same name run in the order in which they were created; the static suites are created before <computeroutput>main()</computeroutput> starts, so they run before the added suites with the same name;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>the order of static suites with the same name, defined in different source files, is not specified; give them distinct names.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</simplesect>
To set the order explicitly, start the names with zero-padded numbers (<computeroutput>"01 - Init"</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>"02 - Run"</computeroutput>). Even so, write each suite so that it does not depend on the others having run before it.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Examples</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="comment">//<sp/>In<sp/>a<sp/>header<sp/>shared<sp/>by<sp/>the<sp/>source<sp/>files.</highlight><highlight class="normal"></highlight></codeline>
